<a name="Intro"></a>
<h1>Introduction</h1>
This example shows how an application that manages its own memory with
<a href="https://github.com/LLNL/Umpire">Umpire</a> can have Ginkgo allocate
from the very same pool.

The application does the entire Umpire setup: it picks the memory resource,
creates a `QuickPool` over it, and registers that pool under a name of its own
choosing. It then builds an executor with the matching Ginkgo allocator --
gko::UmpireCpuAllocator, gko::UmpireCudaAllocator or gko::UmpireHipAllocator --
which looks the pool up by that name in Umpire's `ResourceManager`. From then
on every Ginkgo allocation is served out of memory the application has already
reserved, and the application sees Ginkgo's usage in Umpire's own statistics.

The reason to share a pool is that the alternative wastes device memory: an
application pool sized to the device leaves Ginkgo nothing to allocate, and
sizing both of them to half the device wastes whichever half is idle.

The example solves a 2D Poisson problem with CG and then checks what the pool
saw: that it really served Ginkgo's allocations, that they came out of memory
the application had already reserved rather than growing the pool, and that
nothing was left behind once the solve went out of scope.

<h3> Notes </h3>

Ginkgo must be configured with `-DGINKGO_BUILD_UMPIRE=ON` for this example to
be built, and the application has to link Umpire itself: Ginkgo links it
privately and does not re-export it.

Umpire's pools are not stream-ordered, so sharing a device pool across streams
needs an `Executor::synchronize()` before freed memory is handed out again.
